cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this verifier, or cite the Signed Containment Evidence
  specification it implements, please cite it using the metadata below.
title: >-
  Signed Containment Evidence: specification and reference verifiers
abstract: >-
  A specification and four reference implementations for checking, independently
  and offline, that a compute workload was contained and then destroyed. Nothing
  has to be asked of whoever produced the evidence. An AuditProof carries an
  eight-step SHA-512 chain over the destruction sequence and an Ed25519
  attestation over a fixed canonical view of the document, serialised with
  RFC 8785.

  A verifier answers two questions that often get treated as one. Integrity asks
  whether the document is internally consistent and unmodified, which can be
  answered from the document alone. Authenticity asks whether the key that signed
  it belongs to the party it names, which cannot. A document can be perfectly
  self-consistent and signed by a key nobody vouches for, so the exit codes keep
  the two answers apart. The Rust and Go implementations are checked against a
  100-case conformance corpus by test suites in the repository, and the Python
  and TypeScript ports are written to be byte-equivalent to the Rust one. The
  corpus is generated from a committed generator, so its expected verdicts can
  be reproduced instead of trusted.

  Three properties are requirements rather than intentions. None of the four
  implementations carries a network dependency, so offline verification is a
  property of the code and not a promise about it. A verifier that does not
  implement a document's declared signing mode has to reject the document rather
  than report a partial result, which closes a downgrade an attacker would
  otherwise pick. And the wire form, meaning the eight steps, their order, the
  two algorithms and the canonical view, does not change, because evidence kept
  for years has to stay checkable by the algorithm that produced it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Arra
    given-names: Chaitanya Kumar
    affiliation: Nanorix Inc.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nanorix-io/nanorix-verify"
